+/-!
+# `char::is_uppercase` and `char::is_lowercase`
+
+core/src/char/methods.rs (rustc 1.95.0-nightly efc9e1b50 2026-02-06, `char::UNICODE_VERSION` 17.0.0):
+
+    pub const fn is_uppercase(self) -> bool {
+        match self { 'A'..='Z' => true, c => c > '\x7f' && unicode::Uppercase(c) }
+    }
+    pub const fn is_lowercase(self) -> bool {
+        match self { 'a'..='z' => true, c => c > '\x7f' && unicode::Lowercase(c) }
+    }
+
+`unicode::Uppercase` / `unicode::Lowercase` are the Unicode derived properties, looked up in the
+bitset tables of core/src/unicode/unicode_data.rs. The model is the set of `char`s for which the
+native method returns `true`, as inclusive code point ranges in increasing order: the tables
+below were produced by enumerating the method over every `char` (U+0000..=U+10FFFF without the
+surrogates) with that rustc, and merging adjacent code points. The fixture
+verification/char_case_models.rs checks natively that the same enumeration still has the digest
+that its Lean checks compute from these tables, so a table that differs from the native method
+at any code point fails the fixture.
+-/
